so it is today the 18th anniversary of  9/11 it's incredible it has actually  been more than half of my life has been  lived after 9/11 then before 9/11 which  is incredible it's also incredible just  how fast time passes and with each  passing year since 9/11 since the  program has existed I have been  increasingly unsure I guess I would say  or kind of just ambivalent about what  should we be doing should we just be  doing the same thing every 9/11  anniversary more or less I don't know  it's actually been a few years since  we've even talked about it because for  the last several anniversaries I just  didn't really have anything new to add  so what I want to think about today is I  will retell the story of where I was on  9/11 which I remember vividly  although behavioral economists and  others would say my memory of this has  deteriorated over the years and it is no  longer accurate but you can go back and  check contrast with the first time I  told the story and then I also want to  talk about the sort of legacy of 9/11 is  I I now see it so on on the morning of  9/11 I was in high school and I was  sitting in psychology class with a  teacher of mine who I really like Donald  Palmisano who sadly is no longer with us  he actually passed away not that long  ago and I got the chance to see him out  in western Massachusetts at an event a  few years ago and I remember his wife  actually came up to me having heard my  description of my 9/11 morning and said  you know what when I heard you tell that  story I asked him is it do you think it  really went down that way and he said  you know I don't remember but it sounds  sort of like how it would go down so I  was sitting in psychology class and a  student who was sort of a regular  troublemaker whose name I forgot I think  his name might have been Erik and it's  amazing that his that the students name  I forgotten he was always out of a seat  and always walking around in the halls  he had gone to the bathroom and he came  back into the room  agitated an interrupted psychology class  and said to mr. Palmisano  we're under attack  happening in New York things are crazy  more or less just saying we were under  attack the country's under attack and  mr. palmesano looked at him in the  context of this is a guy who's always  interrupting class and sort of a  troublemaker and not particularly  well-behaved and just didn't believe it  and said you know what I'm sure  everything's fine please just sit down  and of course we later found out that it  was true and I don't think that there  was early dismissal but I certainly went  home and was just watching TV  the rest of the day and the following  day mr. Palmisano actually apologized  through the student and said you know  what you were right I apologize for how  I reacted and it's it's horrible what's  happened and we just had a conversation  in psychology class about what had gone  on since then with each passing year in  some senses our perspective on what took  place changes and evolves partially just  because when things are less recent our  point of view is altered I don't think  any of us can control this also  partially because we know more about  what the legacy has been and for me  unfortunately the legacy of 9/11 in the  United States has been pretty bad we  started a war in the wrong place as a  result of 9/11 against people who didn't  do the thing that we claimed was the  reason for starting the war we have seen  pervasive persistent and also  ineffective privacy invasions as a  result of 9/11 under the guise of  national security we've seen the Patriot  Act we've seen NSA surveillance all of  that stuff that we've talked about over  the last many years post 9/11 that the  David Pakman show has been going on we  have become really accustomed to the  persistent state of the global war on  terror the permanent war status quo of  we're always somewhere for many years  it's been Afghanistan a presence in Iraq  that never seems to end  other potential engagements all sort of  under this post 9/11 mentality of we've  got to make sure  that people out there who want to harm  us aren't able to do it and that we're  projecting strength and all of this  stuff one of the elements of the legacy  of 9/11 sort of the the high points not  in terms of them being morally good or  bad but just notable was the Obama  administration capture of Osama bin  Laden and then of course subsequently  everything we learned about the lead-up  to that and the people that were  involved we've seen a legacy of  continued ignorant and it's really more  than ignorance it's really more of a  deliberate kind of burying our heads in  the sand many of us about what creates  radicalism through foreign policy and  this is not you know very often when we  talk about foreign policy creating  radicals who want to attack the United  States it becomes a very sort of  Manichaean white/black thing of listen  either people are responsible for  choosing to attack the United States or  you're saying that they lack all  responsibility and it's just the fault  of the US through foreign policy for  radicalizing people and of course the  answer is no that's not the black and  white scenario that anyone who's  thoughtful is trying to lay out one of  the things we've hopefully learned over  the 18 years since 9/11 is that  radicalization both domestically and  geopolitically whether we're talking  about white supremacy or Islamic  extremism or whatever the case may be is  a combination of circumstances  domestically which are influenced by the  foreign policies of superpowers like the  United States combined with the  particular predispositions and sort of  circumstances of individuals all of the  white supremacists and former white  supremacists I've interviewed on the  show have told a very consistent story  about how they know who is sort of ripe  for radicalization and indoctrination  and in the same way when we talk about  al Qaeda or Isis or whatever we're not  excused  the actions of individuals when we say  we should consider the role that the  relationships between countries and  American foreign policy have on creating  an environment ripe for radicalization  while understanding that millions tens  of millions hundreds of millions  billions of people on the planet are not  apt to be radicalized regardless of  foreign policy these things are not  black and white and hopefully if there's  a positive legacy from 9/11 and I know  that for a lot of people this just  doesn't work but hopefully the more  thinking people are aware of this stuff  hopefully one of the positive legacies  of 9/11 if there's anything to find in  it is that we have sort of deepened our  understanding of the interaction between  personal circumstances personal  responsibility and geopolitical status  quo in creating radicalization around  the world we also have seen continued  neoconservative war hawking on a lot of  the right and even on some of the left  as a result of 9/11 in terms of even  justifying attempted or would-be  engagements in other parts of the world  over the last 18 years airport security  and the establishment of the TSA of  course a major legacy of 9/11 and  unfortunately not one of the best ones  TSA failing to identify banned items in  almost 95% of internal self-tests drills  so to speak of their security processes  so sadly it is mostly negative there was  I mean if we want to find something that  was sort of good that happened after  9/11 that of course doesn't outweigh any  of the bad there was actually a  short-lived positive sort of sense of  unity in the United States where hyper  partisanship was mitigated a little bit  and it was about coming together as a  country and helping people in New York  City and in Washington DC and all of the  people affected by 9/11 and it didn't  last long and it's not surprising that  it was these sort of black swan' type  events to take a term from Nassim Taleb  who has written about such events that  really kind of shook up the status quo  it didn't last very long and of course  then the administration of George W Bush  choosing to retaliate to 9/11 by going  into Iraq which had nothing to do with  it  did not help at all my question to you  how should we be remembering 9/11 as we  get closer and closer to 20 years out  from the event which is just incredible  that almost twenty years have gone by  and what do you see as the legacy of  9/11 on geopolitics on United States  foreign policy domestic issues and the  status quo I want to hear from you
 

